{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"daring-pancreatic-larva","creation_channel":"cli","description":"Three evaluate() paths in the MCP<->library parity gate ship untested. Raised as non-blocking\nadvisories by the LLM code review on Gerrit 2503 and deferred there; filed here rather than dropped.\n\n1. STALE DIVERGENCE. A declared divergence whose two surfaces have since CONVERGED must fail, so the\n   now-pointless declaration gets removed rather than lingering as a permanent waiver. The path\n   exists in _check_divergences but nothing exercises it.\n2. merge_declarations carry-forward. The commit s central safety claim is that --update carries\n   existing reasons forward and writes an EMPTY-reason stub for a newly-diverging tool, so it can\n   never launder a real divergence into silence. That behaviour is asserted nowhere.\n3. _check_normalization. Neither a schema_version change nor a change to\n   normalization.library_only_params between live and manifest is covered, so the category-level\n   rule that keeps 35 tools quiet could drift unnoticed.\n\nEach is a pure-function case against the already-injectable evaluate(live, manifest), so no new\nharness is needed. See scripts/check_mcp_library_parity.py and tests/unit/test_mcp_library_parity.py.","id":"8d66-52ad-b66c-485a","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Cover the parity gate's untested evaluate paths"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788330573454627001,"uuid":"f9946bb5-9b01-4736-976d-d8b0fdb6bb35"}